# Terraform CLI config, TWO consumers (rec-2514 / Decision 119 reversal mechanism; PLAN-ci-provider-
# mirror-terraform-init-hardening extended the second consumer below):
#   1. CC-web ADMIN sessions, via the every-session hook .claude/hooks/session_start_terraform_assets.sh (bin/sync-terraform-assets.sh).
#   2. GitHub-hosted terraform-validate (.github/workflows/ci.yml), which renders its own resolved
#      copy directly in the job -- decoupling the required, branch-protection-gating check from
#      github.com provider-asset availability.
#
# Routes the kislerdm/neon provider (terraform/personal) through a local filesystem_mirror seeded
# from s3://agent-platform-data-lake/tf-provider-mirror/ (see
# .github/workflows/terraform-provider-mirror-seed.yml) instead of a direct github.com checksum
# fetch, which the CC-web outbound proxy 403s (Decision 119) and which an upstream github.com
# outage can independently 5xx regardless of proxy. hashicorp/* providers still resolve direct
# from releases.hashicorp.com, which neither the proxy nor this mirror concern affects.
#
# Deliberately NOT renamed despite serving two consumers now -- a rename would orphan the path
# named verbatim in Decision 120's ratified body and in terraform/CLAUDE.md (the forward-direction
# amendment convention forbids editing a ratified Decision's original text to chase it).
#
# __TF_MIRROR_DIR__ is a literal placeholder, NOT a shell variable -- each consumer substitutes it
# into its own resolved copy of this file before TF_CLI_CONFIG_FILE is exported. This committed
# copy is never edited in place at runtime by either consumer.
provider_installation {
  filesystem_mirror {
    path    = "__TF_MIRROR_DIR__"
    include = ["registry.terraform.io/kislerdm/*"]
  }
  direct {
    exclude = ["registry.terraform.io/kislerdm/*"]
  }
}
